1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:08,350
This is Nativeproof, a research workspace for testing how a campaign may be received in another culture before money is spent on it.

2
00:00:08,350 --> 00:00:12,162
A team brings a product, a campaign and a target market.

3
00:00:12,162 --> 00:00:29,187
Nativeproof finds cultural signals in Qloo's affinity data, rehearses the campaign with a labeled synthetic audience, flags cultural risks, adapts the copy, compares the versions with a fixed scoring rule, and assembles a report in which every claim points back to its source.

4
00:00:29,187 --> 00:00:37,075
Everything in this video was recorded on the deployed app on October twenty-ninth, with live Qloo data and live language models.

5
00:00:37,075 --> 00:00:40,325
The synthetic participants are generated by AI.

6
00:00:40,325 --> 00:00:44,625
They are not real people, and the app says so wherever they appear.

7
00:00:48,438 --> 00:00:50,950
First, how the system is built.

8
00:00:50,950 --> 00:00:58,288
The web app is Next.js, and it talks to a FastAPI service over HTTPS with a bearer token.

9
00:00:58,288 --> 00:01:05,963
Its API types are generated from the service's OpenAPI contract, so the two sides cannot drift apart silently.

10
00:01:05,963 --> 00:01:16,950
Inside the API, routes check ownership, a run engine executes long jobs in the background, and each job is a LangGraph workflow with four named stages.

11
00:01:16,950 --> 00:01:25,213
Model calls go through one gateway with strict output schemas, while scoring, tallies and rule checks are plain deterministic code.

12
00:01:25,213 --> 00:01:30,350
Qloo is reached only from the server, and everything is stored in PostgreSQL.

13
00:01:31,662 --> 00:01:44,149
In production, the web app runs on Vercel, the API runs on a free Render web service in Singapore, and Supabase provides sign-in, the PostgreSQL database and private file storage.

14
00:01:44,149 --> 00:01:49,874
An uptime monitor calls the health endpoint every five minutes, so the free service stays awake.

15
00:01:49,874 --> 00:01:54,262
Provider keys exist only as server environment variables.

16
00:01:54,262 --> 00:01:59,262
The browser never sees a Qloo key, a model key or a database password.

17
00:02:03,082 --> 00:02:06,369
We sign in with the judging account listed in the README.

18
00:02:06,369 --> 00:02:15,819
Google sign-in is on the same page, and Try the demo starts an anonymous session on clearly labeled fixture data, which never calls a live provider.

19
00:02:15,819 --> 00:02:18,182
This whole session uses live mode.

20
00:02:19,498 --> 00:02:23,623
All three sign-in paths end in one Supabase session.

21
00:02:23,623 --> 00:02:37,235
The API does not trust the browser: it verifies every token itself against Supabase's public signing keys, checks the expiry, audience and issuer, and uses the token's subject as the user id.

22
00:02:37,235 --> 00:02:43,835
Every project belongs to one owner, and a request for another user's data gets a not found response.

23
00:02:47,654 --> 00:02:56,016
We create a live project for a fictional brand, Trailhead Coffee, which is launching canned cold brew for weekend hikers in Japan.

24
00:02:56,016 --> 00:03:02,104
The brand kit records the brand's voice, terms that must stay word for word, and terms that are not allowed.

25
00:03:02,104 --> 00:03:06,791
Then we add the campaign copy and the cultural references we want to investigate.

26
00:03:06,791 --> 00:03:14,641
Every edit creates a new, immutable content version, so each later result names exactly which copy it tested.

27
00:03:18,450 --> 00:03:20,700
This is the path a campaign takes.

28
00:03:20,700 --> 00:03:23,800
Atlas discovers cultural evidence for a market.

29
00:03:23,800 --> 00:03:27,950
Audience rehearses the campaign with synthetic participants.

30
00:03:27,950 --> 00:03:37,250
Voice writes market adaptations, Loop evaluates the original against the candidates under one scoring version, and Proof assembles the report.

31
00:03:37,250 --> 00:03:42,325
Brand Memory is written only by people and is read by every step.

32
00:03:43,626 --> 00:03:46,788
Here is how a Qloo result becomes evidence.

33
00:03:46,788 --> 00:03:54,663
Inputs are validated, and terms that look like an email address or a phone number are refused before anything leaves the server.

34
00:03:54,663 --> 00:03:57,251
Only an allowlist of workflows can run.

35
00:03:57,251 --> 00:04:08,651
Each response is normalized into evidence records that keep the retrieval mode, the retrieval time and hashes of the request and response, and live results are cached for a limited time.

36
00:04:08,651 --> 00:04:13,463
When a term matches several candidates, nothing is chosen automatically.

37
00:04:17,278 --> 00:04:20,815
Atlas resolves the campaign's concepts against Qloo.

38
00:04:20,815 --> 00:04:24,615
In this live run, all five terms came back ambiguous.

39
00:04:24,615 --> 00:04:31,490
Qloo holds several tags named Hiking, for example as a hobby, an activity and a lifestyle.

40
00:04:31,490 --> 00:04:33,640
Nativeproof does not guess.

41
00:04:33,640 --> 00:04:37,990
It lists the candidates, and we confirm the intended one with Use this.

42
00:04:37,990 --> 00:04:44,203
Each choice reruns discovery, and each run took between thirty and sixty-six seconds.

43
00:04:44,203 --> 00:04:47,303
With all five confirmed, ten signals resolved.

44
00:04:47,303 --> 00:04:58,378
The market brief keeps source data apart from model interpretation, every finding cites its evidence, and its risks state plainly that affinity data does not prove demand.

45
00:05:02,186 --> 00:05:09,411
A population study builds on a study, so we create one with two research questions and run the quick rehearsal first.

46
00:05:09,411 --> 00:05:16,848
It generated twelve synthetic reactions grounded in ten discovery evidence records, in fifty-six seconds.

47
00:05:16,848 --> 00:05:25,186
It already raised something we had not considered: several reactions were unsure how hikers would carry the can and dispose of it on the trail.

48
00:05:28,998 --> 00:05:32,348
A population study works in three steps.

49
00:05:32,348 --> 00:05:38,048
A model proposes audience groups, and each group must cite the Qloo evidence it is built from.

50
00:05:38,048 --> 00:05:45,548
Participants are then sampled into those groups with a fixed random seed, so the same population can be interviewed again later.

51
00:05:45,548 --> 00:05:52,173
Interviews run in batches, and every completed batch is saved as a checkpoint before the next one starts.

52
00:05:55,978 --> 00:05:57,428
Now the main study.

53
00:05:57,428 --> 00:06:11,165
We build a new synthetic population of one hundred participants for Japan at standard interview depth, and we upload the ad creative, so participants react to what a vision model actually observes in the image, not to a guess about it.

54
00:06:11,165 --> 00:06:17,215
The study runs in the background, reports its stage and batch, and can be cancelled or resumed.

55
00:06:21,014 --> 00:06:26,301
The study finished in two minutes and twenty seconds, and all one hundred interviews completed.

56
00:06:26,301 --> 00:06:34,326
Seventy-one percent were positive, twelve percent mixed and seventeen percent neutral, with none negative or confused.

57
00:06:34,326 --> 00:06:39,264
The participants come from four groups built from the Qloo evidence, twenty-five in each.

58
00:06:39,264 --> 00:06:49,376
Every number here is a tally of stored interviews, not a model's estimate, and the recommendations name their own gaps, such as the absence of negative voices.

59
00:06:53,178 --> 00:06:59,403
The reaction graph links the campaign's elements to the groups, their reactions and the themes they raised.

60
00:06:59,403 --> 00:07:05,953
Each edge carries a stored count and a definition of what the count means, and selecting a node highlights its links.

61
00:07:05,953 --> 00:07:19,603
Below, every interview can be read in full: the participant's group, reaction and comprehension, each answer to the eight questions, and the label that says it is an AI-generated reaction, not a real participant.

62
00:07:23,422 --> 00:07:26,184
The social simulation runs in rounds.

63
00:07:26,184 --> 00:07:36,884
In each round, a share of participants reads a feed of peer posts and interviews, chosen with a fixed seed, and then posts, comments, shares or ignores.

64
00:07:36,884 --> 00:07:44,147
Each action stores the stance before and after, the stated reason, and the exact feed items the participant saw.

65
00:07:44,147 --> 00:07:50,284
That is what makes every claimed influence traceable, and each completed round is a checkpoint.

66
00:07:54,098 --> 00:07:55,998
We run three rounds.

67
00:07:55,998 --> 00:08:02,423
In each one, twenty percent of the participants read a feed of five peer items, then act.

68
00:08:02,423 --> 00:08:09,473
The simulation completed in seventy seconds, with nine, four and eight stance changes across the rounds.

69
00:08:09,473 --> 00:08:15,773
Each post shows the stance before and after, the participant's reason, and the posts it reacted to.

70
00:08:19,590 --> 00:08:22,602
The diffusion graph shows how content moved.

71
00:08:22,602 --> 00:08:32,890
Each arrow from one audience group to another counts the feed items written by the first group that were shown to the second in this round, and thicker lines carry more items.

72
00:08:32,890 --> 00:08:37,927
Earlier-round posts and the participants' interviews appear as sources too.

73
00:08:37,927 --> 00:08:46,327
Selecting a group highlights its links, and the panel lists each connection with its count, its definition and the stored field it comes from.

74
00:08:46,327 --> 00:08:48,715
The view is labeled illustrative.

75
00:08:48,715 --> 00:08:50,927
It is not a forecast of real reach.

76
00:08:54,730 --> 00:08:58,042
Participants keep a memory of what they said and saw.

77
00:08:58,042 --> 00:09:06,117
A follow-up question is answered from those stored memories, the answer must cite which memories it used, and it is saved as a new memory.

78
00:09:06,117 --> 00:09:11,992
Questions to a whole group go to a seeded sample, and the tally covers only that sample.

79
00:09:15,806 --> 00:09:21,868
This participant's current stance comes from round one, and its memories are its interview and its own post.

80
00:09:21,868 --> 00:09:25,981
We ask a follow-up about carrying the empty can back down the mountain.

81
00:09:25,981 --> 00:09:29,993
The answer cites one stored memory and is saved as a new memory.

82
00:09:29,993 --> 00:09:37,143
We can also question a whole group: a seeded sample of five answered, and the tally covers only those five.

83
00:09:40,962 --> 00:09:44,012
Every model call goes through one gateway.

84
00:09:44,012 --> 00:09:51,024
Each task has a configured model and a minimum capability, and outputs must match a strict schema.

85
00:09:51,024 --> 00:10:00,137
Invalid output gets a bounded repair attempt, provider errors get bounded retries, and a fallback provider is used only when it is enabled and capable.

86
00:10:00,137 --> 00:10:08,537
Usage and latency are recorded per stage, which is how the report can list the model and prompt version behind every step.

87
00:10:12,358 --> 00:10:16,158
Risk Radar scans the copy and the image together.

88
00:10:16,158 --> 00:10:21,033
A vision model, MiniCPM-V, analyzed the uploaded creative.

89
00:10:21,033 --> 00:10:27,345
In twenty-seven seconds the scan found five potential issues, one moderate and four low.

90
00:10:27,345 --> 00:10:36,358
The moderate one is the line, crack one open at the summit, which the model reads as a possible clash with etiquette at sacred peaks in Japan.

91
00:10:36,358 --> 00:10:43,083
The quoted text is checked against the copy in code, and the reading is labeled as model interpretation.

92
00:10:43,083 --> 00:10:49,933
Applying the suggested change creates a new adaptation; the campaign is never edited in place.

93
00:10:53,738 --> 00:10:59,563
Voice writes market adaptations, grounded in the discovery evidence and the rehearsal findings.

94
00:10:59,563 --> 00:11:03,188
It produced two variants in about fifteen seconds.

95
00:11:03,188 --> 00:11:14,163
Each change shows what it replaces, why, and whether evidence supports it, and deterministic checks confirm that protected terms were kept and prohibited terms were avoided.

96
00:11:17,982 --> 00:11:28,019
Loop scores the original and three candidates under one scoring version, from synthetic ratings on clarity, brand fit, cultural relevance and risk.

97
00:11:28,019 --> 00:11:33,782
The best candidate scored ninety point five against eighty-five point eight for the original.

98
00:11:33,782 --> 00:11:41,694
Because the margin is under five points, the decision rule recommends validating with real people instead of declaring a winner.

99
00:11:45,514 --> 00:11:48,876
Loop can also compare versions on identical participants.

100
00:11:48,876 --> 00:11:55,176
We saved the risk fix as version three and interviewed the same one hundred participants again.

101
00:11:55,176 --> 00:11:59,526
Reusing the population skipped all Qloo and construction work.

102
00:11:59,526 --> 00:12:05,501
The result is honest: positive reactions fell from seventy-one to sixty-six percent.

103
00:12:05,501 --> 00:12:12,939
The fix may remove a cultural risk but cost some emotional pull, and the app states that no statistical significance is claimed.

104
00:12:16,742 --> 00:12:25,354
Long work runs in the background with a clear lifecycle: queued, running, then completed, partial, failed or cancelled.

105
00:12:25,354 --> 00:12:31,442
Population studies and social simulations save a checkpoint after every batch or round.

106
00:12:31,442 --> 00:12:35,142
Cancelling stops at the next checkpoint and keeps the finished work.

107
00:12:35,142 --> 00:12:43,842
If the server restarts, a startup sweep marks unfinished runs as interrupted, and runs with a checkpoint can be resumed from where they stopped.

108
00:12:47,658 --> 00:12:53,195
Here we start another study on the same population and cancel it after the first batch.

109
00:12:53,195 --> 00:13:00,120
It stops at the next checkpoint and keeps what was finished: forty of one hundred interviews, saved and readable.

110
00:13:00,120 --> 00:13:04,495
A cancelled run is final, because the person chose to stop it.

111
00:13:08,302 --> 00:13:12,964
Resume is for runs that a restart interrupted, and this is a real one.

112
00:13:12,964 --> 00:13:19,877
We start a study on the same population, and twelve seconds later we restart the production API on Render.

113
00:13:19,877 --> 00:13:24,939
The new server's startup sweep finds the study unfinished and marks it interrupted.

114
00:13:24,939 --> 00:13:32,577
The page says the server restarted during the run, that completed work is saved, and offers Resume from last checkpoint.

115
00:13:32,577 --> 00:13:39,127
Eighty-one interviews were saved; nine that were in progress when the old server shut down are recorded as failed.

116
00:13:39,127 --> 00:13:40,114
We resume.

117
00:13:40,114 --> 00:13:47,202
The run continues from its checkpoint instead of starting over, and finishes with ninety-one completed interviews.

118
00:13:47,202 --> 00:13:53,339
The nine failed ones are named and excluded from every tally, rather than quietly filled in.

119
00:13:57,146 --> 00:14:03,408
Campaign Arena puts concepts side by side for the same synthetic audience and the same criteria.

120
00:14:03,408 --> 00:14:14,983
In eighty seconds, variant B led with ninety-three point eight out of a hundred, and the original was marked as within five points of the leader, so the result is close and is shown that way.

121
00:14:18,798 --> 00:14:25,398
Cultural DNA turns the discovery evidence into a versioned cultural profile in under thirty seconds.

122
00:14:25,398 --> 00:14:35,448
Every item cites its Qloo source, items without support are labeled as hypotheses, and conflicts between signals are listed instead of smoothed over.

123
00:14:39,250 --> 00:14:46,125
Market Expansion runs the same discovery in two to five markets and lays the evidence side by side.

124
00:14:46,125 --> 00:14:53,525
We compare Japan and South Korea for canned cold brew coffee, with the terms hiking and cold brew coffee.

125
00:14:53,525 --> 00:14:58,500
All three terms come back ambiguous, so at first both markets are unavailable.

126
00:14:58,500 --> 00:15:05,562
The ambiguous terms are listed once, above the markets, with the same Use this buttons as Atlas.

127
00:15:05,562 --> 00:15:13,737
Qloo's tag matching does not depend on the market, so one confirmation applies to every market, and the comparison runs again with it.

128
00:15:13,737 --> 00:15:17,625
We confirm hiking as an activity, and both markets complete.

129
00:15:17,625 --> 00:15:30,175
Then we confirm cold brew coffee, and each market shows four resolved signals with opportunities, risks and gaps, while the product category, which we left unconfirmed, stays listed.

130
00:15:30,175 --> 00:15:32,612
The page records the matches we confirmed.

131
00:15:32,612 --> 00:15:38,862
The signals are identical in both markets, so the page says plainly that they cannot rank the markets.

132
00:15:42,662 --> 00:15:50,212
Brand Memory stores the rules every workflow reads, such as the voice and the protected and prohibited terms.

133
00:15:50,212 --> 00:15:55,324
Trend Watch compares two saved discovery snapshots without calling a model.

134
00:15:55,324 --> 00:16:05,399
It shows which signals appeared and disappeared, and it warns that two snapshots are not a trend line, and that a difference can come from matching changes rather than from culture.

135
00:16:09,210 --> 00:16:25,647
Every result is stored in PostgreSQL with explicit relationships: projects own campaigns, campaigns own immutable content versions, runs record their inputs and stages, and evidence records keep their source, type and retrieval details.

136
00:16:25,647 --> 00:16:31,622
Schema changes go through migrations, and row level security protects user-owned tables.

137
00:16:32,922 --> 00:16:36,584
The Evidence Graph is built only from those stored links.

138
00:16:36,584 --> 00:16:40,372
Every edge names the stored field that establishes it.

139
00:16:40,372 --> 00:16:47,859
A link that was never recorded is never inferred, and large graphs return a bounded subgraph that says it was truncated.

140
00:16:51,670 --> 00:16:54,207
Here is the graph for this session.

141
00:16:54,207 --> 00:17:04,370
Zoomed in, each line is a stored link: a run produced an evidence record, the evidence supports an adaptation, an evaluation evaluates a version.

142
00:17:04,370 --> 00:17:10,220
Selecting a node highlights its connections and lists each one with the field it comes from.

143
00:17:10,220 --> 00:17:17,707
This node is a Qloo source signal produced by a discovery run, with its retrieval details and limitations attached.

144
00:17:21,506 --> 00:17:24,268
Ctrl K opens project search.

145
00:17:24,268 --> 00:17:33,206
It is semantic search over the project's stored evidence, risk findings, Cultural DNA items, adaptations and brand examples.

146
00:17:33,206 --> 00:17:41,143
Each hit shows its type and evidence label, and the similarity score is described as a ranking signal, not a probability.

147
00:17:41,143 --> 00:17:44,418
Opening an evidence hit shows the full record.

148
00:17:48,222 --> 00:17:53,522
Finally, Proof assembles the report in thirteen seconds without calling a model.

149
00:17:57,202 --> 00:18:06,714
Back on Home, the dashboard counts what this session produced: the project, the market researched, the evidence records and the recent completed runs.

150
00:18:06,714 --> 00:18:10,389
Each number is a count of stored records, not an estimate.

151
00:18:11,698 --> 00:18:19,923
Nativeproof helps teams find cultural risks and opportunities before launch, and it says clearly when the evidence is not enough.

152
00:18:19,923 --> 00:18:32,160
Every claim is labeled as source data, calculation, interpretation or hypothesis, and synthetic participants are a tool for forming hypotheses, not for proving them.

153
00:18:32,160 --> 00:18:35,660
Decisions still need validation with real people.

154
00:18:35,660 --> 00:18:39,910
The code, the twelve diagrams and the setup guide are on GitHub.
